--TEST--
`Core\IO::stdin` reads to end of input in one call, and a program started with nothing attached sees the empty string rather than a wait — spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A case is started with its standard input closed, which is also what a
// program run by a scheduler or a service manager sees: end of input arrives
// at once, and the member answers the whole of what arrived, which is nothing.
// The point is that it answers at all -- a member that waited would hang this
// suite rather than fail it.
tainted string $first = Core\IO::stdin();
echo "length=", Core\Str::length($first), "\n";

// Asking a second time is neither an error nor a second half. `stdin` is a
// read to end of input, so once input has ended the only thing left to answer
// is what has arrived since, and nothing has.
tainted string $second = Core\IO::stdin();
echo "again=", Core\Str::length($second), "\n";
if ($first == $second) {
    echo "same=yes\n";
} else {
    echo "same=no\n";
}
--EXPECT--
length=0
again=0
same=yes
